      "part": "problem",
      "original_record": "before",
      "value": "Let $I=N$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{-i,\\,0,\\,i\\},$ then ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=$",
      "original_html_sha256": "c7e3e618c72793d46847be45e2589b4e752a15f2d1caff6475f30a9952e36d5b",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071758/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/c7e3e618c72793d46847be45e2589b4e752a15f2d1caff6475f30a9952e36d5b.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    },
    {
      "part": "worked_solution",
      "original_record": "history",
      "value": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}=\\{-1,0,1\\}$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${X}_{2}=\\{-2,0,2\\}$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${X}_{3}=\\{-3,0,3\\}$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that all the integers belong to one of the sets.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "original_html_sha256": "d0cef85dba2fa6acd5a91562fdc1c3623a73fee3ac720588868e42110c192206",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
